window.docContents[116].resolve({"/Terms/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-API-for-CVC5--Terms--Typed-Terms":{"contents":"A cvc5 term whose sort is the one the Lean type α denotes.Term Int, Term (Set Bool) and Term (Int → Bool) are terms of the SMT-LIB sorts Int,\n(Set Bool) and (-> Int Bool); a Lean type denotes a sort through its ToTyp instance. Every\nconstructor states its sorts through the index, so a sort mistake is a Lean type error rather than\na cvc5 error at runtime, and a value read out of a model comes back as an α.The index is phantom: at runtime a Term α is exactly a Cvc.Untyped.Term. erase goes down for\nfree, and Cvc.Untyped.Term.typeCheck goes up by checking the term's sort.\n\nTyped terms have a type parameter for the actual Srt of that term. We saw previously that ToTyp links types to Srt-s, and it is precisely the mechanism used here. Typing terms lets us check statically that term constructors are invoked in a valid way, and makes their signature as precise as it can be.\n\nA boolean literal.\n\nBinary conjunction.\n\nAn integer literal.\n\nSome term operators are polymorphic: addition for instance works on any arithmetic terms as long as they have the same sort.\n\nBinary addition.\n\nNotice that the type-class constraint IsArith α must hold, which asks for a proof that the Typ associated to α is actually arithmetic.\n\nCarries a proof that the Typ associated to α is arithmetic.Proof that α's Typ is arithmetic.\n\nWe can declare function symbols by either letting type inference figure out the type for us, or by providing it explicitly.\n\nA symbol of the given sort: a fresh uninterpreted constant.\n\nA symbol of the sort α describes, naming α explicitly.\n\n\n\n","context":"Cvc.lean -- High-Level API for CVC5\u0009Terms","header":"4.1. Typed Terms","id":"/Terms/#Cvc___lean----High-Level-API-for-CVC5--Terms--Typed-Terms"}});